#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199032} :activity/id #uuid "4b440e9b-6a56-5700-a008-c294f960b104" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049269 :topic/id #uuid "e8b201cc-85fe-5f18-9ad6-6feec356fe49" :topic/math-academy-id 4252} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199518 :tutorial/content "Recall that the statement \"$A$ and $B$\", represented symbolically as $A \\land B,$ is true if $A$ is true *and* $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nWe can represent all possible permutations of the truth values of $A$ and $B$ and the subsequent truth values of $A\\land B$ using a **truth table**:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\land B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nEach row of the truth table contains an input for $A,$ an input for $B,$ and the corresponding output for $A \\land B.$\n\n- The first row says that if $A$ is true and $B$ is true, then $A \\land B$ is true.\n\n- The second row says that if $A$ is true and $B$ is false, then $A \\land B$ is false.\n\n- The third row says that if $A$ is false and $B$ is true, then $A \\land B$ is false.\n\n- The fourth row says that if $A$ is false and $B$ is false, then $A \\land B$ is false." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee98774e-d738-5b66-b2fc-e3c35ee94bf2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7963 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e0c5d2cc-7c60-5845-95d4-cfaea7286f8b" :step/math-academy-id 119963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199600 :tutorial/content "The statement \"$A$ or $B$,\" represented symbolically as $A \\lor B,$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false. \n\nThe corresponding truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nAs we'll see, truth tables provide a convenient way to determine the truth values of complex logical statements. They can also be used to prove some mathematical theorems!" :tutorial/id #uuid "69e8a334-8207-5b5e-a725-f336ddf61468" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9048 :tutorial/title "The Truth Table of the \"Or\" Operator"} :step/id #uuid "af5b4487-0954-56d0-967a-c0db892f6793" :step/math-academy-id 119964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198095 :question/id #uuid "e94e320d-c2d0-5ab4-b1e5-4e1e2c667bd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12703"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21fd31e7-1eed-5dad-aff2-691ba1737b92" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048778}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570786}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table"} :step/id #uuid "0440c492-e6e7-5adb-93a0-616d0582fb60" :step/math-academy-id 119965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199601 :tutorial/content "Consider the following statement:\n\n$$\nA \\land (B\\lor A)\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can use a truth table to calculate the truth values of this statement given all possible permutations of the truth values of $A$ and $B.$\n\nFirst, we complete a truth table for the part in parentheses, $B \\lor A.$  The statement $B \\lor A$ is true if either $A$ is true, $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nNext, we add a new column for $A \\land (B\\lor A).$ \n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\nTo fill in the rows, we consider the truth values of $A$ and $(B\\lor A)$ and use this to determine the truth value of $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ in each case.\n\n- Consider the first row. Since $A$ is true and $(B\\lor A)$ is true, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *true*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\n- Next, consider the second row. Since $A$ is true and $(B\\lor A)$ is true, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *true*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |  |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\n- Now, consider the third row. Since $A$ is false and $(B\\lor A)$ is true, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *false*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |  |\n\n- Finally, consider the fourth row. Since $A$ is false and $(B\\lor A)$ is false, their conjunction $A \\land (B\\lor A)$ is *false*:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nThe completed truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,B \\lor A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land (B \\lor A)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "9540705e-7502-5456-93aa-8987f69089ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9049 :tutorial/title "Truth Tables of Compound Statements"} :step/id #uuid "ea424b90-9a67-54e5-890d-eb71c3cebc21" :step/math-academy-id 119968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198093 :question/id #uuid "4063a7d8-0a49-55c7-9e93-3b7ac58c7343" :question/math-academy-id "e-12679"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ab339-1aa8-51a1-ac86-dc780120657c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570770}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table for a Compound Statement"} :step/id #uuid "f0bf4b65-03a2-5b70-8097-f40d31d39e16" :step/math-academy-id 119969 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199519 :tutorial/content "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth tables. \n\nA trivial logical equivalence is that every statement is logically equivalent to itself. We write this as follows:\n\n$$\nP \\equiv P\n$$\n\nTo prove this, we can use a truth table.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nSince $P$ and $P$ have identical truth values, they're logically equivalent.\n\nAnother trivial equivalence is that the conjunction of a statement with itself is equivalent to the original statement.\n\n$$\nP \\land P \\equiv P\n$$\n\nFor example, the compound statement \"$x=3$ and $x=3$\" is logically equivalent to \"$x=3$\", which matches our intuition. \n\nAgain, we can prove the general result using a truth table.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P \\land P\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nThe statements $P$ and $P\\land P$ are logically equivalent since they have identical truth values. \n\nWe also have the following logical equivalence for the disjunction of $P$ with itself.\n\n$$\nP \\lor P \\equiv P\n$$\n\nWe can justify this result using a truth table. \n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,P \\lor P\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n\nThe statements $P$ and $P\\lor P$ are logically equivalent since they have identical truth values. \n\nCollectively, the results\n\n$$\nP\\land P \\equiv P ,\\qquad P\\lor P \\equiv P\n$$\n\nare known as the **idempotent laws.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "94d94958-eb9c-560a-8751-b05351806f2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7976 :tutorial/title "The Idempotent Laws"} :step/id #uuid "aeed77e3-d9c7-5a50-b4a2-1ca4802d3d55" :step/math-academy-id 119972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198094 :question/id #uuid "e46add42-b23d-59cc-a611-a2f1b282d3bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12701"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe7854c-d438-5ee0-adc8-c32d5d800050" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570764}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Compound Statement"} :step/id #uuid "01942453-2001-5f5f-bec4-23750d4bbbe8" :step/math-academy-id 119973}] :activity/title "Truth Tables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198512}]}
